#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Comps: rendered from the contract, generated by a model, and measured after.

Three separable jobs, and keeping them separate is what makes this trustworthy:

  1. RENDER   A comp drawn deterministically from `.deluxui/design.contract.yaml`.
              Needs no model, no key and no network, so it is always available.
              It is conformant by construction -- every fill is a declared role,
              every size a declared rung, every radius a declared value -- which
              means it cannot be the thing that drifts.
  2. GENERATE A raster from an image model, when one is reachable. The prompt is
              built FROM the contract rather than from an adjective, and the
              result carries its prompt, provider and the contract's hash inside
              the file.
  3. VERIFY   The generated raster measured back against the contract it claims to
              express. This is the half a generator alone never does: a model
              returns a beautiful image in a palette nobody declared, somebody
              approves it, and the built screen is then judged against a comp that
              was never the system. Drift is reported per role, in numbers.

    ux_image.py brief --write [--surface "property detail"] [--options 3]
    ux_image.py render BRIEF.yaml [--out .deluxui/comps]
    ux_image.py generate BRIEF.yaml [--provider auto] [--model flash]
    ux_image.py prompt BRIEF.yaml [--option A]
    ux_image.py verify comp.png [--contract PATH]
    ux_image.py providers

Exit codes: 0 fine, 2 findings (drift, or a brief that cannot be rendered),
3 no generation provider is reachable (a real NOT_RUN, never a silent no-op).
"""
from __future__ import annotations
